  I do this exercise on paper before I open any screen. The tactile separation matters — it forces me to think about work rather than tools. My current go-to is the Leuchtturm1917 A5 notebook, which I’ve used for every planning session for two years running. There’s something about the resistance of a good notebook that slows thinking down in exactly the right way.
  
  [_→ Check it out on Amazon_](https://amzn.to/4cma022)
  
  **2\. Pick one research tool and make it your second brain**
  
  Research is where most knowledge workers hemorrhage time. Open tabs, bookmarks, half-read articles, notes scattered across three apps — the average freelancer spends more time managing information than using it.
  
  One tool should own your research layer. For me, that’s NotebookLM. Everything I read that matters goes in. Every source for every article I’m writing lives there. When I sit down to write, I’m not searching — I’m drawing from a curated, queryable library I built during the research phase.
  
  The key word is one. If you use two research tools, you will spend your time wondering which one has the thing you’re looking for.
  
  **3\. Pick one generation tool and build your system prompts inside it**
  
  Your generation tool — the one you use for drafting, ideation, and first-pass creation — should be the tool where your system prompts live. Not a separate document. Not a Notion page. Inside the tool, saved, ready to load.
  
  I use Claude for generation. My system prompts for every content type I produce are saved as Projects. When I start a new article, I open the relevant project and the context is already there: my voice, my formatting preferences, my constraints, my series arc. The prompt is the infrastructure. The conversation is just execution.
  
  **4\. Pick one publishing tool and stop redesigning it**
  
  Notion, Obsidian, Bear, Craft — every six months someone releases a new note-taking and publishing tool and the productivity community migrates en masse. Resist this entirely.
  
  Your publishing tool is not where your thinking happens. It’s where your finished thinking lands before it goes out the door. The tool doesn’t matter. The template inside it does. Build a strong template once, stop tweaking it, and treat redesigning it as the procrastination it actually is.
  
  I run everything through Notion. I haven’t changed my template in four months. That stability is the point.
  
  **5\. Add one scheduling and batching layer**
  
  The most underrated part of any AI productivity stack is not a tool — it’s a decision about when you use the tools. Batching similar tasks dramatically reduces the cognitive overhead of context-switching.
  
  I have four fixed work blocks per week: research, drafting, editing, and admin. AI tools operate differently in each block, with different system prompts and different objectives. This structure means I’m never in a meeting mindset trying to do deep research, and never in a research mindset trying to produce finished drafts.
  
  Protecting these blocks physically matters. I use a Time Timer on my desk — a visual countdown that makes the block tangible and creates a natural stopping point that a phone timer never quite does. It’s a small thing that changes the quality of the work meaningfully. [_→ Check it out on Amazon_](https://amzn.to/4tRjCI2)
  
  **Where This Goes Wrong**
  
  Adding tools to fix process problems. If your output is inconsistent, adding a new AI tool won’t fix it. Inconsistency is a systems problem, not a capability problem. Fix the system first.
  
  Optimizing the stack instead of using it. There is a category of person who spends more time reading about productivity tools than producing anything with them. The stack is not the work. The stack exists to disappear.
  
  Upgrading before you’ve gone deep. The marginal gain from switching to a new tool is almost always smaller than the marginal gain from using your current tool 20% better. Go deep before you go wide.
  
  Letting novelty masquerade as necessity. Every major AI platform releases impressive new features constantly. Not one of them has changed my core stack in four months. Updates are not reasons to rebuild.
  
  **The Stack in One Sentence**
  
  NotebookLM for research. Claude for generation. Notion for structure. Calendar blocks for protection. Everything else is optional until proven otherwise.
  
  The measure of a good stack is not how many tools are in it. It’s how rarely you think about it while you’re working.
  
  I use the Anker USB-C hub to keep all of this running off a single laptop port — one cable, all peripherals, nothing unplugged mid-session. It sounds trivial until you’ve lost twenty minutes of focus to a charging issue.
  
  [_→ Check it out on Amazon_](https://amzn.to/4cCEc7U)
  
